  "title": "Kourovka Notebook Problem 21.105",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "A group word $w$ is said to be concise in a class $\\mathcal C$ of groups if for every group $G$ in $\\mathcal C$ such that the set $G_w$ of word values of $w$ in $G$ is finite, the verbal subgroup $w(G)=\\langle G_w\\rangle$ is also finite. Is every word concise in the class of residually finite groups?",
